everyone this is Peter Brown standing in  for freelance reviewer Daniel Starkey  for their early access review of for  tonight almost every moment of fortnight  is a chaotic mess for better and for  worse it's an action-packed shooter that  knows how to encourage cooperation  between team members and as a result  you'll often build out hasty force with  friends to defend yourselves from hordes  of cartoonish beasts that part is  usually a thrill and highlights all the  best pieces of what should be a solid  formula building basis with friends to  defend against monsters  however the reality of that is selling  far more often than it should be by a  mesmerizing deluge of content for  starters for tonight isn't even an easy  game to describe it staples together  pieces of disparate genres into  something entirely new combines the  construction elements and resource  gathering of Minecraft the team-based  shooting of left4dead  and Gears of War the quest design of a  modern MMO and the progression of any  given free-to-play hit it's surprising  that such divergent elements work  together at all  but fortnight definitely knows what its  best at and tries to thrust you into  ideal scenarios early on fortnight is  quite content to keep its writing in  tone rooted firmly in the realm of the  comedic instead of any serious moves to  address the story behind an apparent  zombie invasion you get a solid stream  of jokes instead  [Music]  fortnight's undead look about as  threatening as a Furby a bit creepy in  the right lightfoot otherwise harmless  if you're trying to bust down a wall  it'll pop and flex with each strike as  if ripped from a Looney Toons skip every  character you find will be some  over-the-top caricature of a classic  disaster slash horror movie trope in  your main companion for example is a  finicky bot that only partially knows  how to run your shelter that task is  ultimately up to you as you organize  expeditions to gather other wacky folks  to join your band all while seeking out  whimsical tech such as sky lasers to  help cleanse the world of ghouls early  missions teach you the basics of defense  and shooting after that you're tossed in  with three or four other players and  told to stand fast against wave after  wave of foes you make friends and  coordinate on some basic plans to help  safeguard the base this isn't optional  either you'll be running lots of  instances with other players you won't  know you will need their help though  most missions are challenging attacking  the things on your own generally isn't  advised tearing through zombies with  others is so easy to get right but it's  here that fortnight sets itself apart  the shooting is sharp and tight but that  feeling to clean or artificial  similarly tight communication with the  squad can help you focus on problem  monsters worming their way through your  defenses you could have someone set a  trap in a weakened area or have a friend  buttress a wall as you Clea charge out  and hurl grenades unfortunately for us  all after a few minutes that mode ends  and you enter what amounts to advanced  inventory management these missions with  others are means to several ends they're  how you gain experience and progress  your character they're how you gather  materials you'll need to make new  weapons and build out your base they're  also where you will rescue and then  retrieve survivors together all these  little elements plus plenty of even  smaller ones become the other main  thrust of fortnight monsters  at first pass these interconnected  pursuits make for tonight feel great  it's a prime example of how game working  itself around a few core ideas that  conspire to give you a lot to do but  that's just it  most of these are the end result of  missions that you play leaving combat  heavy areas is nice for a breather but  it also contrasts with all the other  systems highlighting their weaknesses  for tonight is designed to be  free-to-play but for now at least it  isn't it shouldn't be a big of a deal as  it is but it's impossible to get around  much of the game is designed to burn  time you have energy meters in the form  of research that your pack of survivors  conducts these meters place limits on  progression in that there are resource  you must wait to accumulate at the same  time they're engineered to keep you  coming back since you can only store so  many points before they need to be  collected at this point if you have  substantive experience with mobile  free-to-play games you can see what's  going on here for tonight loves giving  you extra things to do and stats to pour  over there are stats for each of your  different heroes for the defenders for  the research team is your personal  inventory and progress that's tied to  your account and then there are also  levels for each of your characters and  you can get dozens of these each of them  can be trained outfitted and upgraded  and then placed into teams that you can  align based on personality types for  maximum statistical benefit the problem  is that most of these activities aren't  interesting in and of themselves instead  of fleshing out all these individual  ideas for it it only gives you the  option to crunch for better numbers all  of these elements are linked to so if  you're not keeping up in one piece the  logistics of your operation will screech  to a halt for lack of one resource or  another for completionists that will  certainly have an appeal but others may  drop the grind currently content  dominates fortnight there's so much to  do so many skills to unlock heroes to  find quests to finish and llamas to  whack and this can cause for name to  choke on itself at times when it feels  focused however it makes a grand case  for itself there is nothing quite like  scrambling to coordinate with your team  to build out some extra turrets as you  all blast away in a last-ditch effort to  save a mission these moments for now at  least just don't come as often as they  need to the good news though is that for  tonight's issues are solvable they're a  matter of balance and tuning and  expanding upon what's there the game is  currently in early access status with a  full release plan for 2018 at that point  that will also become free-to-play so  check back then for GameSpot's updated  take on for tonight's unique mix of  looting crafting and shooting  Lama I love helping the good guys  [Music]
 

